# The curriculum expert: a standing reviewer

One role (set up by Cory, 2026-11-11), used whenever the plan needs an informed outside view: a scope and sequence, a unit plan, an
assessment, a shape call, a rule in SPEC.md, or a question like "is five days right for this standard". It reviews,
guides and analyses. It never builds, fixes specs or edits the thing it is judging.

Run it as a fresh agent every time, with no part of the working conversation, so it is not anchored on what we
already think. The Claude Code definition is `.claude/agents/curriculum-expert.md`; it points here.

## Who it is

A curriculum reviewer with the working knowledge of three people: a classroom teacher of the grade in question, a
district curriculum lead who has sat on adoption committees, and a researcher who knows what the learning
progressions and the evidence say. It is on the side of the student and the teacher, not of the plan.

## Before it judges anything

The job brief you were given (docs/pipeline/briefs/) overrides this page where they differ, above all on what a
blind read may not open.

0. **Read the subject lens first:** docs/agents/lenses/<subject>.md for the subject in hand (one file per subject: the four core subjects and each later subject, health, physical-education, arts, computer-science and the rest).
1. **Read the bar we hold ourselves to:** docs/FOUNDATION.md (what may not be contradicted), docs/PRODUCTION-PATH.md
   (the build order and what each layer must hold), the subject's rubric file (docs/RUBRIC-MATH-K8.md,
   docs/RUBRIC-MATH-HS.md for high school math, docs/RUBRIC-ELA.md, docs/RUBRIC-SCIENCE.md) or, for a subject with no rubric file, docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md
   (docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md is the wider summary), the BASELINE rules (from rule 20 on), and the standard text in
   data/standards/.
2. **Read the thing under review in full**, and the files it points at.
3. **Teach itself from outside sources, and name them.** Its own memory is a starting point, not evidence. For math:
   the standards' own progressions documents, the publishers' criteria and focus documents, the rubric's evidence
   guide, the scope and sequence of two or three well-reviewed open curricula, and research summaries on the
   topic (the What Works Clearinghouse practice guides, for one). For other subjects, the equivalents. Where a
   source cannot be reached, it says so and marks what rests on memory.
   **Start from what earlier runs already found.** The expert keeps no memory between runs, so
   what it learns has to be left in the repo. Before searching, read:
   - docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md: about 75 sources with working addresses, licences, how to reach each, the "Evidence
     notes" (what each source was found to say, with sizes and limits), and the ranked list of sources nobody has
     read yet. Paywalled sources stay unread and nothing may rest on them (settled 2026-11-11, see DESK-ANSWERS).
   - docs/APPROACH.md and the subject page: the approach every scope and sequence, unit plan and section plan must follow.
   - docs/courses/math-3-5-progression.md (grades 3 to 5 math; an older page, data/progressions/math.csv is current):
     handoffs, limits and page pointers already checked against the Progressions. Point to it, and re-check only what
     the review at hand depends on. Not on a blind read whose brief forbids it. **Not on a progression read:** a
     progression is read without the subject's earlier course pages (docs/courses/, earlier
     progression and course reviews), so the read is not anchored on them; the reconcile step
     (docs/pipeline/briefs/progression-reconcile.md) compares them (settled 2026-11-13, see DESK-ANSWERS).
   - docs/reviews/: every earlier report. Read the ones on the same course or layer; do not repeat a settled
     finding, and say so when a new read disagrees with an old one.
   How to open a PDF: WebFetch returns binary. Download with curl into a new empty folder in the scratchpad and
   extract the text with `python -I -X utf8` (pypdf, or pymupdf where fraction numerals or margins drop out), with
   scripts kept in a different folder. Page pointers are written "PDF page (printed page)".
   **Leave it better.** If a run reads a source that is not in docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md, or finds a new address or
   a way past a block, it says so at the end of its report under "For the source list", and the caller files it.
4. **Respect licences.** It reads and links. It does not copy text into the repo, and it never says Curio Class was
   reviewed by, meets or is endorsed by anyone.

## How it reports

One file in docs/reviews/, named `<what>-<date>.md`, and the same text back to whoever called it.

- **Verdict in three sentences**, first: is it sound, what is the biggest risk, what one change matters most.
- **Findings, most serious first.** Each has: what is wrong or at risk, the evidence (a source and a link, or
  "from memory"), what it costs a student or a teacher, and a concrete change. No finding without a change.
- **What is right and should not be touched**, briefly, so a later edit does not undo it.
- **What it could not judge**, and what it would need.
- **Sources**, as links, with one line on what each was used for.
- **Confidence** on each finding: firm (two or more sources agree), likely (one source or strong reasoning),
  or a judgment call (reasonable reviewers differ).
- **Settled by the expert**, under that heading: see the next section.

## What it settles, and what goes to Cory

Settled 2026-11-11, see DESK-ANSWERS.

- **The expert settles item-level choices inside the standards' limits:** number sizes, which pairs (which
  fraction pairs, for one), and day swaps inside a unit. It does not send these to Cory. It lists each one in
  its report under the heading "Settled by the expert": the choice, the reason in one line, and the file it
  touches. The caller applies them; the expert still edits nothing.
- **A weekly digest for Cory:** the caller copies every "Settled by the expert" entry into docs/reviews/DIGEST.md,
  newest week first, so Cory can read a week's choices in one place and reverse any of them.
- **Still Cory's:** anything that changes scope, audience, money or outside people, and anything that moves a
  standard between sections or units. These go to Cory on the Review Desk.

## Rules it keeps

- It is constructive: every criticism comes with a way through that fits Curio Class (free, no accounts, a small
  team, tools plus print).
- It says when the plan is right. It does not pad a review to look thorough.
- It separates what an adoption reviewer would mark down from what is merely its own taste.
- It does not soften a finding because the work is recent or was just approved.
- When an exit, item or practice set could be read as doing its job or as not doing it, it reads it as a busy
  writer and a student would, not at its best, and reports the fault if the likely reading fails (lead 2026-11-13,
  see DESK-ANSWERS).
- Severity follows the cost: a fault a writer would build wrong days from is serious however small the fix; a count
  that does not add up is minor unless the right number cannot be told from the draft.
- Plain words, no em dashes, "Curio Class" never shortened. A finding that changes what a course teaches goes to
  Cory on the Review Desk; the caller files it, the expert does not. Item-level choices inside the standards'
  limits are the exception: the expert settles those (see "What it settles, and what goes to Cory").

## Where it is used in the path

- The approach to teaching (docs/APPROACH.md and each subject page), before Cory's yes and whenever it changes.
- Every scope and sequence and every unit plan, beside the Codex read, before the fix pass.
- The section plan fields on their first three sections, then a sample of each course's section plans.
- The probe and guard days, beside the fresh-agent read against the rubric (it may be the same run).
- Any Shape call marked a judgment call, and any change to the teaching test or the three hooks.
- At the end of a course: the whole-course read (item 9 of "What a finished course holds").
- On request, for guidance before work starts, which is cheaper than a review after.

## Before reading a course plan

Run `python -X utf8 scripts/plan.py check`. If it fails, the plan goes back to its writer unread. Start from the
unit's reading pack (`scripts/plan.py pack <course> <unit>`). Do not spend the read on arithmetic the
script has done; spend it on what the script cannot see (data/plans/README.md, "What the gates cannot see").
Log each finding with `scripts/plan.py defect ...`, found_by `expert`, so the ledger shows what each read earns.
# Lens: arts

Pre-work, 2026-11-16. The expert reads this before any arts course frame, scope and sequence, unit plan or day
in visual arts, music, theater, dance or media arts. It is a way of seeing, not a bar: the bar is the arts part
of docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md. The standard and the band-year rule are in docs/courses/arts/STANDARD.md
(National Core Arts Standards, 2014, ids only). The approach page is docs/courses/arts/APPROACH.md (approved 2026-11-20).

## What good teaching of the arts looks like

**Four processes, every course.** The standards sort the arts into creating, performing (presenting in visual
arts, producing in media arts), responding and connecting. A good year moves through all four, and most units
touch three. A year of only making, or only appreciation, is not an arts course.

**Technique is taught, not hoped for.** Expression grows from skill. The teacher names a technique, shows it in
steps, and has students practise it with feedback before they use it in their own work. The steps get harder
across the units and across the years: a K to 2 line becomes a contour drawing becomes a gesture study.

**The discipline's words do work.** Each discipline has its own words (value, tempo, blocking, levels, framing).
They are taught on the day they are needed, then used when making and when talking about work. A response with no
discipline words is an opinion, not arts learning.

**Students make their own work and show it.** Copying a model is a step, not the end. Each unit leaves room for
choice in what is made or studied, and ends with the work shown or performed to someone. The showing can be
small: the class, a partner class, a family evening, a hallway wall.

**Critique is a routine, not a free-for-all.** Feedback follows a set protocol: describe what you see or hear,
ask, then suggest, tied to the unit's look-fors. Students revise after feedback and say what they changed.

**Works from many times and places, with their setting.** Each year draws works from more than one culture and
period, and from living artists as well as old ones. A work is taught with when, where, by whom and for what,
not as a picture on a slide. No single tradition is the default.

**Assessment watches growth.** Kept work over time (a portfolio, a recording log) shows growth better than a
single score. Finished work and performances are scored with a guide that names the technique and the words.
Students assess themselves against the same guide.

**The room matters.** Arts happen in real rooms with real limits: one or two sessions a week, a cart instead of
a studio, thirty students and one set of drums. Good plans fit the session, list the equipment, set up and clean
up inside the time, and say how to use the space safely.

**The performing is in the room.** A screen can show and explain. It cannot rehearse a scene or a dance with a
class. Plans put the doing with people and the tool in a supporting role.

## Errors reviewers catch

### Scope and sequence

- A grade's ids placed in the wrong year of the band, or a high school level tied to a grade (STANDARD.md 3).
- A year with one artistic process missing.
- Works studied all from one tradition or all from one century.
- Technique that does not get harder across units or years.
- A plan that needs a daily period in K to 8.
- No showing in a unit.

### Unit

- A making task with no technique taught first ("express your feelings in paint").
- Response work with no discipline words, or with words that were never taught.
- A work studied with no setting: no time, place, maker or purpose.
- Critique with no protocol, or feedback that is praise only.
- No revision after feedback.
- An end-of-unit assessment that is a vocabulary quiz instead of a making, showing or response task.
- A unit that copies the coalition's enduring understandings, essential questions or cornerstone tasks (they
  need permission; we write our own).

### Day

- Set-up and clean-up that do not fit the session.
- Equipment the room may not have, with no low-cost option.
- Unsafe use of space (dance and theater: room to move, floor, footwear; visual arts: blades, heat, fumes).
- A student who must perform alone with no other way to take part.
- The word "respond" used for the artistic process on a respond day: a respond day acts on a check (GLOSSARY).
- A tool asked to judge a finished work or a performance.

### Across the arts

- The five disciplines planned as one: each has its own techniques, words and works.
- A dance unit that double-teaches what PE teaches in its dance units, or a music unit that teaches fractions
  instead of leaning on math (STANDARD.md 6).

## What this lens does not cover

- Whether a particular work is suitable for a grade or community: the course page's local note and the values
  rule apply.
- Instrument and voice pedagogy at depth (a qualified music teacher's call).
- Copyright of the works studied: the open material scout and each source's own terms.

## Sources, read into our own words

- National Core Arts Standards, 2014: the four processes and eleven anchors (the free part), and the at-a-glance
  handouts behind our id file, with the high school music composition and theory strand handout (read 2026-11-21).
  Copyright guidelines read 2026-11-16 (STANDARD.md 2).
- The arts associations' opportunity-to-learn standards, for recommended weekly time (STANDARD.md 3.4, read
  2026-11-21).
- NCAS "Standards Process Updates for the Field", March 2026: the revision's five aims.
- NAfME, appendix to the 2014 music standards, the descriptors for the three high school levels.
- The bar's instruments, as read by the bar's writer: Texas fine arts rubric (2025), California's five categories,
  Oklahoma's arts rubric (2024). Not reread for this lens.
- From memory, not opened for this lens: the "studio habits of mind" research on how visual arts classes teach
  (envision, observe, reflect, stretch and explore and others) and the common critique protocols (describe,
  analyze, interpret, judge). Treat these as background, not cited claims.
# Course frame: Visual Arts, grades 9 to 12

Course `visual-arts-grades-9-to-12` (catalog id `visual-arts-9-12`), a band course for high school, run as four levels one after another (Level 1, Level 2, Level 3 and the optional Level 4), each a full year, named by level and never by grade (STANDARD.md 3.2). Draft frame, written 2026-11-25; the scope and sequence sets the days later, one level at a time, and each level's part of this file is that level's frame.

## The course in brief

- **Units.** 19: five in each of Levels 1, 2 and 3, four in Level 4. Levels 1 to 3 share one shape, so a thread
  comes back one level harder in the same place: an opening unit (Level 1's bridge, the review unit in Levels 2 and
  3), then a looking and materials unit, an ideas and revision unit, a design and judging unit, and a curating and
  museum unit that ends the year with the level's show. Each of the four paired units joins two progression threads,
  one of creating and one of responding, presenting or connecting, so every unit makes, studies other artists' work
  and shows (bar criteria 4 and 5). Level 4 is one sustained body of work in four units, one for each Level 4 row:
  the proposal and series, the critical essay, the project with a purpose, and the exhibit (APPROACH "High school
  levels and music").
- **Days.** 640: 160 a level for four levels (Level 4 optional; a school that offers three levels uses 480). Each
  level: 156 days in its units and 4 for course-level checks and respond days (a mid-year check and a year-end check,
  each with one respond day). One lesson of 45 minutes a day, five a week, 32 weeks a level (STANDARD.md 3.4: a high
  school level is 160 course days of 45 minutes, the about 120 hours the national Proficient descriptor names; the
  80-day short path is 60 hours). The 45 minutes hold the set-up routine (about 3 minutes), the teaching and making,
  and clean-up and collecting work (about 7 minutes in an ink, paint, clay or printing session, 3 in a drawing,
  writing or talk session); a retrieval task takes ten minutes. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment
  and, in units over 25 days, its mid-unit assessment, each with a respond day. Unit counts follow the rows' load:
  the design and judging unit is longest in Levels 1 to 3 because it holds a team or solo build with a written
  rationale and a written judgment; the bridge and review units are short because they own no id. These are course
  days: the school calendar (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) runs them one a school day, so course day n of a level falls
  in that level's week n / 5, rounded up. National time figure: the visual arts educators' association's high school
  checklist has not been read at source, so no association figure is stated here (STANDARD.md 3.4: a figure not read
  at source stays off the page; For the lead item 5).
- **The 80-day short path (a semester or a rotation).** Each level's scope and sequence marks an 80-day path that
  keeps every unit and every id by merging the marked days (STANDARD.md 3.2). Days on that path: Level 1, units of
  6 (the bridge), 15, 16, 20 and 19; Levels 2 and 3, units of 3 (review), 16, 17, 20 and 20; Level 4, units of 25,
  14, 19 and 18; each level's two checks keep 1 check day and 1 respond day. Checks fall in week 8 (mid-course) and
  week 16, the half-year finals week. Each unit keeps, in this order and no fewer: the demonstration with a step card,
  opened by the look at a work that carries the unit's response and its connecting line; the making days with the
  one critique and the revision; the mid-unit and end-of-unit assessments; the respond days. Fluency days fold into
  the openers, and the two rows' understanding days merge where the scope marks them. A merged day never drops a
  critique, a revision or a respond day. The bridge's 6 short-path days are 7.5 percent of 80, inside the bar's 10
  percent limit on days outside the course's standards (12 of 160 on the full path, the same share).
- **Levels, entry and placement.** STANDARD.md 3.2 binds. Level 1 is Proficient (`.Ia` ids), Level 2 Accomplished
  (`.IIa`), Level 3 Advanced at honors depth (`.IIIa`), Level 4 an optional year the student leads, assessed again
  on `.IIIa` with new, self-directed work. A student new to visual arts starts at Level 1 in any grade. Each level
  needs the one before, or a placement showing: the student's work scored against the previous level's year-end
  check, met at the review unit (units 6 and 11). Advanced is claimed by assessed work, not by finishing Level 3; a
  student who ends Level 3 at "partly" has Level 4 to reach it. One level is a complete course. Choice grows by level
  (APPROACH): Level 1 sets the problems, Level 2 lets the student pick among them and research, Level 3 has the
  student set the problem, Level 4 is the student's own proposal.
- **Standard.** National Core Arts Standards, 2014, visual arts performance standards, by id only, each named with
  its line in data/standards/ncas-performance-standards-words.txt, never quoted (STANDARD.md 2); each id's action
  was checked against the national standard's own text for this frame. Level 1 claims the 15 `.Ia` ids, Level 2 the
  15 `.IIa` ids, Level 3 the 15 `.IIIa` ids: 45 in all, the count in STANDARD.md 3.2, every one taught and assessed in
  its own level. Level 4 claims no new id: its rows contribute to the `.IIIa` ids, which it assesses again on new work.
  No grade 8 id is claimed (the bridge teaches grade 8 techniques and words as readiness). Every level touches all
  four artistic processes (creating, presenting, responding, connecting; bar criterion 1), and each unit touches at
  least two. In the Units table the seventh column is headed "Processes, anchors and works". Check set: each unit
  plan checks itself against the words-file lines of its ids, the nine criteria of the arts part of
  docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md and the lens's error list (docs/agents/lenses/arts.md); the "Check set adds" column
  names what that is expected to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds: on political and social works,
  facts and backed sources decide claims, people are taught with dignity, and a student's own view is never scored,
  only the evidence and reasoning behind it. scripts/standards.py checks every id against the id file; each level's
  scope and sequence still states its id count (15) against STANDARD.md 3.2.
- **Base text.** None. Visual arts is Curio Class's own (docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md; the open-material scout,
  docs/reviews/arts-open-material-2026-11-16.md, found material to study, not plans to follow). Works studied come
  from museum open-access collections and public domain government collections (public domain works released by the
  holding museum or library) and, for living or recent makers, from a description in our own words with a link to
  the maker's or museum's page, never a copy (APPROACH "Rights"). Each work named below is a pick the unit plan keeps
  unless its rights line, checked on the item's own page, fails; a swapped work keeps the unit's ids met and the
  level's range. Across the band the works come from Africa, the Americas (with four Native American makers or
  Native-led shows: units 5, 9, 13, 18 and 19), Asia, Europe and Oceania, from about 420 BCE to now, with living
  makers in every level (APPROACH "Works and their settings"); open images lean to older European and American works,
  so the list was built for range. Curio Class writes every step card, work card, hazard card, reading, case card,
  record sheet, guide and family note.
- **Techniques that get harder (bar criterion 2).** Drawing: value scales, contour and gesture, one-point
  perspective (the bridge, as grade 8 readiness), then thumbnails and observational studies (Level 1), sustained
  studies over a term (Level 2), a planned series (Level 3), a self-set series (Level 4). Wet media: ink and
  watercolor worked without a plan (Level 1), mixed media weighed old and new (Level 2), a series in one chosen
  medium (Level 3). Form and space: a scale model of a school space in card (Level 1), a redesigned object or place
  model (Level 2), a work about material culture (Level 3). Printmaking: foam-plate and collagraph prints, no blade
  cutting (Levels 1 and 2). Presenting: a small show and a kept collection (Level 1), a sited portfolio show with
  hanging and lighting chosen (Level 2), a curated event show with justified choices and a conservation plan (Level
  3), a public exhibit with labels and a talk (Level 4).
- **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md ("teaching day") and APPROACH "Kinds of day". Understanding: a technique shown
  in named steps with the step card, or a work studied with the unit's words (the artistic process "responding" is
  taught here, never on a respond day); first teaching is always understanding. Fluency: short repeated drills with
  feedback (a two-minute gesture set, a value strip, a thumbnail page, a mixing chart, a looking routine on a new
  image); in high school it is the five-minute opener on most days and five or six fluency days a unit. Application:
  making and writing with some choice, drafting and editing included; the second making week holds the unit's one
  critique (describe, ask, suggest), and the revision days after it are application too. Check: the mid-unit and
  end-of-unit assessments and the course-level checks. Respond: a second try at the part marked "not yet". A paired
  unit of 32 to 40 days runs roughly: 9 understanding, 6 fluency, 14 to 20 application with the critique and the
  revision, 1 mid-unit check and 1 respond, 2 end check days, 1 respond.
- **Scored evidence.** Every owned id has an end-of-unit item or a look-for on the unit's guide, named id by id in
  the unit's "End-of-unit evidence" line. A progression row's "Assessed by" note names one form for the row, not one
  item for each id, so it is never the whole line. Items come in four kinds: a look-for on the product's guide (three
  to five look-fors, met, partly or not yet), scored from the kept work after the session; a kept page made on the
  unit's making days (a planning portfolio entry, a hazard card, an idea log, a research page), scored with the
  product; a written item in a sitting on an unseen open-licensed work or a Curio Class original; and the artist
  statement or written rationale, drafted, given written feedback and revised before it is scored. Each paired unit
  has two end check days: day one is a sitting of written items on unseen works (set-up 3 minutes, 38 minutes of
  writing, collecting 4); day two is the showing, where each student self-scores on the guide and the class gives
  the showing's audience notes, and the product is scored from the kept work afterwards. A check day has no paint,
  ink or clay, and one adult watches one room. The product's draft, its critique with the teacher's written marks on
  the record sheet, and its revision come on earlier days counted in the unit. No score comes from the day a technique
  or a work is first taught. The mid-unit assessment (one check day and one respond day inside every paired unit)
  scores the unit's planning or materials item while the product is still in progress. Each unit opens with a
  baseline look at its main look-for during the first practice days (APPROACH "Growth"), so every student has two
  marks a unit and the growth is the change. One piece a unit goes into the portfolio with a one-line written
  reflection (bar criterion 8); the portfolio is the Level 2 portfolio show's source and Level 4's starting point.
- **Readiness and retrieval.** Level 1 assumes no earlier arts learning (STANDARD.md 3.2). Its unit 1, the bridge,
  teaches the grade 8 techniques and words Level 1 needs to every student, as readiness, and claims no grade 8 id; it
  stands in for a readiness check, since the grade 8 rows were last used one grade back for a student who took Visual
  Arts, grades 6 to 8 straight through, and two or more grades back (or never) for a student who starts later. Units
  2 to 5 then open with a retrieval task inside day 2's opener on the bridge's technique and the grade 8 row in the
  same thread (taught the same year in the bridge), and day 1 stays clean for the hook and the question. Levels 2, 3
  and 4 assume the level before, taken the year before (the count starts from the band's order: a student who takes
  one level a year). Their units open with a retrieval task inside day 2 on the level before's row in the same thread.
  A student who placed in, or skipped a year between levels, meets the level before's techniques again in the review
  unit (units 6 and 11), whose placement sitting stands as the readiness check; Level 4 has no review unit, so a
  student returning after a gap takes unit 16's first checkpoint as its readiness check (its ten minutes on the last
  day of unit 15's respond work for a student continuing). The Seams tables give each row's last use.
- **Course-level checks.** Grades 9 to 12 have one rule: week 32 is the finals week (CALENDAR.md stagger rule 4).
  Each level's mid-year check is in week 16, at the semester's end (course days 79 and 80 in Level 1, 77 and 78 in
  Levels 2 and 3, 79 and 80 in Level 4); its year-end check is in week 32, the finals week (days 159 and 160). The
  curating unit's show, in weeks 25 to 31, is its own end-of-unit assessment, and the year-end check samples the
  units before it.
- **Grade 12 calendar.** Any level may be taken in grade 12 (Levels 3 and 4 most often). Its last weeks hold
  teaching: the curating or exhibit unit runs to week 31 and the year-end check sits in week 32. In a school whose
  seniors leave before week 32, a senior sits the year-end check on the school's senior exam day (week 30 or 31), and
  the curating unit's last fluency days and two of its making days fold (up to five days, marked in the scope); the
  show, its critique, revision and respond days never fold. A Level 4 senior's exhibit opens by week 30 for the same
  reason. A student of 18 or over gets the family note themselves and gives their own yes to be shown (AGENTS.md;
  va-g12-exhibit); the school's own rules still apply.
- **Leave-out.** A school or family may leave out a unit or a work for its community's reasons (STANDARD.md 5); each
  unit stands alone. Units this course names: unit 15 (it studies Benin royal altar works, a ceremonial use, by
  looking only), unit 14 (war and colonial imagery: Guernica and the Dufour wallpaper) and unit 5 (a museum show built
  around objects of slavery). Swapping a work keeps the unit's ids met; leaving a unit out means the course page and
  the unit's family note name the ids not claimed that level.
- **The ordinary room.** A class of 30 at tables, paper, pencils, charcoal, found material; nothing must be bought,
  and every unit names a free stand-in (STANDARD.md 3.4, APPROACH "Supplies"). One student at home: the parent or a
  sibling is the audience and the critique partner, a team task has a solo version with the same look-fors, and the
  showing is to the family. The teacher needs no art training: each unit's teacher page says what to look for, in
  plain words; a school with a certified art teacher uses that teacher.
- **Supplies and cost.** Provisional cost per unit for a class of 30 (consumables, reusable items such as brushes,
  ink sets and printing brayers counted once, printing of cards and readings), all optional beside the free path:
  Level 1 $15, $45, $15, $30, $15 ($120); Level 2 $5, $40, $35, $25, $20 ($125); Level 3 $5, $35, $40, $25, $20
  ($125); Level 4 $45, $5, $35, $25 ($110). Planning figures, not quotes; the ceiling is under For the lead.
- **Devices.** None per student. One teacher screen may show a work, a step or an exhibition's own pages; every day
  runs from the printed step card, work card and reading without it. Units 3 and 7 offer a photo walk or photo log on
  a phone or a shared school camera, by the student's choice; the paper path does the same with viewfinder thumbnail
  drawings. No owned id needs technology, so a paper-path student meets every id in full. Kept work stays on paper or
  on the student's, family's or school's device (APPROACH "Kept work").
- **Paper path.** Every day runs on paper, objects and printed cards; any Curio Class tool a unit plan adds (a
  color-mixing practice tool, a work viewer, an exhibit floor-plan tool) has a printed version that does the same job
  and needs no student account.
- **Reading load.** Readings are Curio Class's own, at grade 9 to 10 text level in Level 1 and grade 11 to 12 in
  Levels 3 and 4, about 600 to 900 words a reading day; a museum's or artist's own page is read in excerpts the unit
  plan names. English learners: each unit teaches its five or six words in depth, with an image and a frame ("The
  evidence in the work is ___, which suggests ___."), a student may show a technique instead of naming it, and the
  check marks what the student made apart from what they wrote (APPROACH "Supports").
- **Safety read.** Every unit is hands-on, so every unit gets the fresh safety agent's read
  (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md) against two free guides, named here and opened by the agent: the
  federal consumer product safety agency's "Art and Craft Safety Guide" (cpsc.gov) and California's environmental
  health hazard office's guidance on art and craft materials for schools, with its precautions for grades 7 to 12
  (oehha.ca.gov). Rules for every unit, from APPROACH "Safe use" ("from memory" until that read checks them): only
  supplies labeled non-toxic and conforming to the art materials labeling standard; a hazard card (our own words,
  from the maker's safety data sheet) read before each new material; blades used by an adult only, so prints are
  foam-plate or collagraph, never cut lino; water-based media only, no solvents, no aerosol sprays or fixatives in
  the room; no powdered clay or dry pigment mixing (premixed paint, moist air-dry clay); charcoal and pastel dust
  wiped with a damp cloth, never blown; windows or a fan on for ink and acrylic; hands washed after every wet session;
  no heat source, kiln or hot glue in the plans; no food or drink at the work tables; students do not climb to hang
  work, an adult hangs above head height. Each unit's last column gives its hazards and controls in the words
  days.csv will copy. No student sign-off form.
- **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md),
  which covers the works with the rest of the unit; no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Units with a
  cultural, Native, hard-history or political subject name the communities and the sources the agent studies first,
  in the last column: units 2, 5, 7, 9, 10, 12, 13, 14, 15, 18 and 19. Every task that ties art to the student's own
  life (the Cn10 ids in units 4, 9 and 14, and the Level 4 project) offers an invented or public option marked the
  same (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"); a social or political aim is the student's choice, from a list the teacher
  checks, and its side is never scored. Rows that ask it (va-g11-design, va-g11-museum) have a second adult read a
  work on a sensitive subject before it is shown. Each section carries health's steps for "If a student tells you
  something" (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md) on its teacher page.
- **Logic model (one page in the scope and sequence).** Inputs: a 45-minute lesson a day, step cards, work cards,
  readings, found and low-cost materials. Activities: technique taught in steps, works and exhibitions studied with
  their settings, making with a critique and a revision, a show each level. Outputs: a portfolio of four to five
  pieces a level with reflections and artist statements, a show a level, two marks a unit per student on the main
  look-for. Outcome claimed: met marks on the level's 15 ids and growth on the look-fors. Nothing here has been tried
  with students, and Curio Class makes no claim that arts study raises any other result (APPROACH "Sources").

## Seams

### Picked up from Visual Arts, grades 6 to 8

Visual Arts, grades 6 to 8 has no frame written, so this table is taken from the progression's grade 8 rows and the
Level 1 rows' `assumes` cells. Each grade 8 row is last used one grade back for a student who took that course
straight through; the bridge (unit 1) teaches its technique and words to every Level 1 student, and the paired unit
named below retrieves it on day 2.

| Grade 8 row hands on | What grade 8 did | Row that picks it up (Level 1, unit) | What Level 1 does with it |
|---|---|---|---|
| va-g8-ideas | A record of early steps; a group question about daily life | va-g9-ideas (unit 3) | Three start routes and a question of the student's own, with no teacher-set prompt |
| va-g8-craft | A planned risk; images labeled own, open license or public domain | va-g9-craft (unit 2) | Making without a plan; the health and nature effects of materials, with hazard cards |
| va-g8-design | Pictures and text arranged for an audience; group identity art | va-g9-design (unit 4) | A team proposal, drawn and modeled, for one school space, with an idea log |
| va-g8-revise | Named standards examine unfinished work; written change plans | va-g9-revise (unit 3) | Standards from past and present cultures, named as whose |
| va-g8-show | A themed group show with wall stories | va-g9-show (unit 5) | The student as curator of a small show and critic of a real exhibition's layout |
| va-g8-museum | An exhibition's sway on belief; works sorted by group identity | va-g9-museum (unit 5) | Society and politics in an exhibit's analysis; culture's effect on a viewer's reaction |
| va-g8-look | Taste and media settings compared | va-g9-look (unit 2) | A written proposal on how works shape understanding; the student's own seeing |
| va-g8-judge | A written analysis and an argued judgment | va-g9-judge (unit 4) | An interpretation with sufficient evidence; standards a jury would use |

### Inside the band: level to level

Each row of Levels 2, 3 and 4 assumes the level before's row in its thread, taken the year before, so it opens as a
retrieval task inside day 2 of its unit.

| Thread | Level 1 row (unit, band days) | Level 2 row (unit) | Level 3 row (unit) | Level 4 row (unit) | What changes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Ideas | va-g9-ideas (unit 3, days 45 to 78) | va-g10-ideas (unit 8) | va-g11-ideas (unit 13) | va-g12-studio (unit 16) | Start routes, then problems from one's own work, then a plan for social change and a themed series, then a self-written proposal |
| Materials and ethics | va-g9-craft (unit 2, days 13 to 44) | va-g10-craft (unit 7) | va-g11-craft (unit 12) | va-g12-studio (unit 16); va-g12-purpose (unit 18) | Materials' health effects, then skill over a term and the ethics of sharing, then liberty against duty, then a record of ethical choices |
| Design and connecting | va-g9-design (unit 4, days 81 to 120) | va-g10-design (unit 9) | va-g11-design (unit 14) | va-g12-purpose (unit 18) | A proposal for one place, then a redesign for an issue, then a work on how images help or hinder lives, then a project for a real audience |
| Revision | va-g9-revise (unit 3, days 45 to 78) | va-g10-revise (unit 8) | va-g11-revise (unit 13) | va-g12-studio (unit 16) | Other cultures' standards, then one's own vision, then defended changes, then revision repeated on a self-led series |
| Showing | va-g9-show (unit 5, days 121 to 158) | va-g10-show (unit 10) | va-g11-show (unit 15) | va-g12-exhibit (unit 19) | A small show, then a sited portfolio show, then an event show with conservation, then a public exhibit |
| Museums and society | va-g9-museum (unit 5, days 121 to 158) | va-g10-museum (unit 10) | va-g11-museum (unit 15) | va-g12-exhibit (unit 19) | An exhibit's sway analyzed, then links argued, then a curated collection and a judged influence, then a public exhibit's labels |
| Looking | va-g9-look (unit 2, days 13 to 44) | va-g10-look (unit 7) | va-g11-look (unit 12) | va-g12-criticism (unit 17) | How works shape understanding, then how well images sway an audience, then patterns across a type or era, then an extended critical essay |
| Judging | va-g9-judge (unit 4, days 81 to 120) | va-g10-judge (unit 9) | va-g11-judge (unit 14) | va-g12-criticism (unit 17) | Standards set, then others' standards tested, then competing readings and several standards, then a defended essay |

Band days: Level 1 is days 1 to 160, Level 2 days 161 to 320, Level 3 days 321 to 480, Level 4 days 481 to 640.

### Where the threads end

This is the last visual arts course. The four Level 4 rows (va-g12-studio, va-g12-purpose, va-g12-exhibit,
va-g12-criticism) hand on nothing: their `hands_on` cells are empty, and the threads end in the Level 4 portfolio,
the project, the exhibit and the essay. For a student who stops after Level 3, the threads end in unit 15's show and
its year-end check; for a student who stops after Level 1, in the Proficient level, which is a complete course.

