| 1 | Level 1 | The bridge: tools of seeing. *What does an artist need to know before the work begins?* | 12 | | None | Creating (Cr2, practiced as readiness); responding (Re7, practiced). Works: Elizabeth Catlett, Sharecropper (United States and Mexico, 1952, linocut; the unit plan records the holding museum's page and rights line), value built from line; Vincent van Gogh, The Bedroom (France, 1889, the Art Institute of Chicago, open access), color and a tilted perspective | Readiness, claiming no id (STANDARD.md 3.2): the grade 8 techniques and words Level 1 needs, taught to every student. A value scale in pencil and charcoal; contour and gesture drawing; one-point perspective from a school hallway; mixing a color wheel and its neutrals; the sketchbook and practice log set up; the hazard card routine and clean-up; the critique routine (describe, ask, suggest); labeling a borrowed image as own, open license or public domain, with credit (the grade 8 craft row's idea, reviewed). The looking routine (look, describe, interpret) on the two works, each with a work card. Words: value, contour, gesture, perspective, credit, critique. Showing: a value study and a perspective drawing on the class wall. Evidence: no score; a readiness record of each technique (met, partly, not yet) sets unit 2's groups and its reteach, and the baseline looks start here. Out: every Level 1 id (units 2 to 5). Tools: step cards, the hazard card, work cards, a credit-label card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil for every value study (charcoal optional, from a school set), copy-paper sketchbook. Short path: 6 days | First because Level 1 assumes no earlier arts learning and every later unit needs these techniques, the studio routine and the looking routine; it stands in for a readiness check (Readiness and retrieval). At most 12 days, inside the bar's 10 percent limit | Candidate: a value study by a maker from Asia or Africa beside Catlett's, for range | Hazards and controls: charcoal dust wiped damp, never blown; hands washed; pencils and sharpeners only, no blades. Catlett's print shows a farm worker: the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's page and Catlett's own published words first. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
