nit assessment), id by id: VA:Pr4.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the exhibition plan and the show: works chosen and ordered with written reasons, and the kept collection's care notes; VA:Pr5.1.Ia, the kept note on a real exhibition's layout, scored on its guide (two reasons and two ways named, a judgment of whether they work); VA:Pr6.1.Ia, a sitting item: how an unseen exhibit's room plan and labels (a Curio Class original) shape a visitor's awareness of a social or political question; VA:Cn11.1.Ia, a sitting item: an unseen work with a short reading on its culture, the first reaction and how the reading changed it. Mid-unit assessment (day 19): the exhibition plan draft, with a respond day. Out: a portfolio show sited in one place (Level 2). Tools: the exhibit-plan card, label cards, work cards, readings on the two exhibitions and on Jingdezhen porcelain, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box mounts, tape, a hallway wall. Short path: 19 days | Last in Level 1 because the show is curated from the level's kept work (units 1 to 4) and the analysis of real exhibitions uses unit 4's standards for a collection. The year-end check (week 32) follows it and samples units 2 to 4 | Candidate: an exhibition from Africa or Asia whose own pages explain its layout | Mining the Museum showed slave shackles beside fine silver: the unit names it as a show about slavery and its record, the sensitivity agent reads the Maryland Center for History and Culture's and the artist's own published words first, and a student may write on Americans instead. Americans: the sensitivity agent reads the museum's own pages first; the unit studies how images of Native people are used, never imitates them. Ai Weiwei's work touches Chinese politics: facts from the holding museum, no claim beyond the sources. Family yes, or the student's own at 18, for any showing outside class. Hazards and controls: an adult hangs work above head height; no ladders for students; glue sticks and tape. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
