ages to a panel or a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Cr2.3.IIa, a guide look-for on the model and rationale: the issue named, the redesign answering it, the audience; VA:Cn10.1.IIa, a guide look-for on the research page: what was observed, read and tested, each dated; VA:Re8.1.IIa, a sitting item: an unseen work with its label, the kinds of background facts that would change its reading, named and sorted; VA:Re9.1.IIa, a sitting item: two given sets of standards (Curio Class originals) tested against an unseen work, which fits better and why. Mid-unit assessment (day 20): the research page and first drawings, with a respond day. Out: a work arguing how a culture's images help or hinder lives (Level 3). Tools: the design-brief card, the research page, case cards, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box card, tape, found objects. Short path: 20 days | After unit 8 because the redesign uses the planning page and polished revision; judging others' standards builds on Level 1's standards set (unit 4), revisited here. Before unit 10 because the arguments about art and history there need the background-facts list | Candidate: a redesign case from Asia or Latin America, from its own published source | Mirror Shield: made for the 2016 water protectors at Standing Rock; the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own words and a Standing Rock Sioux Tribe or Native-run source first; the unit studies the design, never takes a side on the pipeline (values rule), and no student copies Native designs. Red Star's work is about her own nation's delegation: her own published words first. Issues: the student picks from a checked list or an invented case, marked the same; the side is never scored. Hazards and controls: card and tape, an adult cuts any thick card or board, no mirrors or glass (foil card instead). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
