uide look-for on the portfolio selection: works chosen and critiqued in writing, one left out with a reason; VA:Pr5.1.IIa, the exhibition plan for the named place, scored on its guide (methods weighed, chosen and used, the place's light and traffic named); VA:Pr6.1.IIa, the kept argument, scored on its guide: links argued and defended with sources; VA:Cn11.1.IIa, a sitting item: two unseen images from two past societies (open access) compared for their use, linked to one local use today. Mid-unit assessment (day 20): the argument's draft with written feedback, with a respond day. Out: an event show with conservation, and a judged influence (Level 3). Tools: the exhibit-plan card, a hanging-methods card, work cards, readings on the Roman and Mughal portraits and on wax print's trade history, Curio Class's own. Free path: tape, string and clips, a hallway wall. Short path: 20 days | Last in Level 2 because the portfolio show is chosen from the level's kept work and the arguments use unit 9's background facts. The year-end check (week 32) follows it and samples units 7 to 9 | Candidate: a local mural or monument named by the school, with its own maker's or city's page | Augustus, Shah Jahan and the official portrait are rulers' images: the unit studies how art served power, with facts from the holding museums. Shonibare's work ties to British naval history and colonial trade: the sensitivity agent reads the Royal Museums Greenwich page and the artist's own words first. Local monuments may be disputed: facts and sources decide claims (values rule). Hazards and controls: an adult hangs work above head height and handles any light; no ladders for students. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
