Each check day: the student works alone in a sitting (set-up 3 minutes, 38 minutes of work, collecting 4), with no
paint, ink or clay out; making items use pencil or a dry medium. Every student's evidence is collected once in a
window; no artifact is scored twice. Days above are level days; on the band's count Level 2 starts on day 161, Level
3 on day 321 and Level 4 on day 481. On the short path the mid-year check is in week 8 and the year-end check in
week 16.

## Cross-subject

One visual arts row of this band carries a cross_subject lean (data/progressions/arts.csv): va-g12-criticism leans on
ela:W.11-12.1 (an argument with valid reasoning and evidence). The contacts after it are practiced, not owned, each
with its "if later" line; all are core courses (lesson 32).

### English language arts, grades 11 and 12 (same year, owned lean)

| Used here | Contact | If English has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|
| Unit 17 (Level 4, level days 51 to 78; va-g12-criticism): the critical essay's claim, counterclaims and evidence | W.11-12.1 is taught in english-language-arts-grade-12 unit 2 (about days 16 to 38) and in english-language-arts-grade-11 unit 3 (weeks 7 to 11, days 35 to 55); Level 4 is most often taken in grade 12, and a student who placed ahead may take it in grade 11 | Both units come before unit 17 in the year. If a school's English course runs later, unit 17's first understanding days teach the claim, counterclaim and evidence frame the essay needs, on the essay planner, and the guide scores the art criticism, not the English standard |

### English language arts, grades 9 and 10 (practiced, not owned)

The written interpretations and arguments of units 4, 9 and 10 practice argument and claim-and-evidence writing
(W.9-10.1: english-language-arts-grade-9 unit 6, weeks 20 to 24; english-language-arts-grade-10 unit 4, weeks 13 to
17). If later, the art unit's evidence-sentence frame and the guide carry what the item needs, and the item scores the
art id only.

### Mathematics (earlier grades)

Unit 4's scale model uses a given scale (1 to 50), which leans on grade 7's scale drawings (7.G.A.1), taught two or
more grades before Level 1 (STANDARD.md 6). The art unit gives the scale on a ruler card and asks no scale
computation beyond reading it (lesson 13).

### Social studies (grades 9 to 12)

The works' settings (the Great Migration, the 1930s, Rome, Mughal India, the Kingdom of Benin, the Mexican mural
movement, the Vietnam War memorial) sit near world history and United States history courses. No lean either way: each
art unit teaches the setting it uses itself, on work cards and readings (bar criterion 7), with social studies'
sourcing habit of naming who made a source, when and why.

## For the lead

1. **Check weeks.** This frame's checks sit in weeks 16 and 32 in every level (32 is the 9 to 12 finals week, rule 4;
   16 the semester end). Several after-core 9 to 12 courses also use 16 and 32 (health, physical education); 9 to 12
   has no stagger rule beyond the finals week, so nothing is moved. Left for the lead's one calendar pass.
2. **Level rows on the calendar.** The Year cells read "Level 1" to "Level 4", so frame.py files them under "9-12
   Level n", not a grade. Pick: keep them off the grade 9 to 12 rows, since a level is not a grade (STANDARD.md 3.2).
3. **Blades in high school.** APPROACH "Safe use" says "blades only by an adult" for every visual arts course, which
   rules out linocut and craft knives in 9 to 12, where they are usual. This frame keeps the rule (foam-plate and
   collagraph prints). Pick: keep it until the safety agent's read of the two guides; if Cory wants linocut in Levels
   2 and 3, APPROACH changes in place with a DESK-ANSWERS line.
4. **Two levels in one room.** Many high schools teach Level 1 and Level 2 students in one period. STANDARD.md does
   not say how. Pick (a scope question for Cory): each level keeps its own units and ids, and the two levels' units
   share the opening look and the critique days where their units sit side by side (unit 2 with unit 7, and so on);
   the scope and sequence marks the shared days.
5. **National time figure (a download).** The visual arts educators' association's high school opportunity-to-learn
   checklist (2025 posting on arteducators.org, the same set as the elementary file) was not read: this job allows no
   downloads. Pick: the lead reads it at source and the Days bullet gains the figure and the gap on both paths (225
   minutes a week on the full path; the short path gives the same minutes for half a year).
6. **Shared doc stale (from the pilot, still open).** docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md, arts row of "Before a scope and
   sequence": "Owed: the standards gate (later.csv L83); load the 63 strand ids when the high school music frame is
   written." Both are done (2026-11-21). Replacement: drop both from the owed list.
7. **Supplies ceiling (money, for Cory).** Provisional costs: $120 (Level 1), $125 (Levels 2 and 3), $110 (Level 4)
   a class of 30, all optional beside a free path. Pick: a ceiling of $150 a class a level for 9 to 12 visual arts,
   with every unit's free path kept.
8. **Grade 8 row spelling.** va-g8-craft's words cell has "open licence" (UK); US is "open license". A 6 to 8 row,
   so not in this frame's Row changes; for the 6 to 8 writer or the lead.

