| 2 | Level 1 | How images shape us. *How do the images we see shape how we understand people and the world, and what does a material do when we make without a plan?* | 32 | va-g9-look; va-g9-craft | VA:Re7.1.Ia; VA:Re7.2.Ia; VA:Cr2.1.Ia; VA:Cr2.2.Ia | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2); connecting touched through the works' settings. Works: Jacob Lawrence, The Migration Series (United States, 1940 to 1941, 60 panels held by The Phillips Collection and The Museum of Modern Art), described and linked; Helen Frankenthaler, Mountains and Sea (United States, 1952, on loan to the National Gallery of Art), poured and soaked paint, described and linked; Ibrahim El-Salahi, Reborn Sounds of Childhood Dreams I (Sudan, 1961 to 1965, Tate; born 1930, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), forms grown from calligraphic marks, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the bridge's looking routine and the grade 8 look and craft rows (va-g8-look, va-g8-craft). VA:Re7.1.Ia: write a short proposal on how one set of works (the Migration Series) shapes how people understand a human experience. VA:Re7.2.Ia: analyze, in a written reflection, how images seen every day (a feed, an ad, a poster the student picks, printed or described) shape the student's own view of the world. VA:Cr2.1.Ia: make a work in ink and watercolor, or in moist air-dry clay, without planning it first, and log what the material did. VA:Cr2.2.Ia: explain how traditional and non-traditional materials (pigments, inks, clay, found plastics) can affect people's health and nature, from hazard cards, and handle them safely. Words: perceive, experience, analyze, unplanned, hazard, ventilation. Showing: the unplanned works with their log pages, on the class wall. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Re7.1.Ia, the kept written proposal, scored on its guide (a claim about understanding, evidence from three panels, their setting); VA:Re7.2.Ia, a sitting item: an unseen open-licensed image, how seeing it could shape a viewer's view of the world, with the student's own case; VA:Cr2.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the unplanned work and its practice log: worked with no plan, what the material did named twice; VA:Cr2.2.Ia, a sitting item: from an unseen hazard card, one health and one nature effect and the safe handling steps, and a guide look-for on handling observed on the rotating list during the making days. Mid-unit assessment (day 16): the hazard card item for the unit's two materials, with a respond day. Out: judging how well images sway a named audience (Level 2). Tools: work cards, hazard cards, a reading on the Great Migration's art, a practice log page, Curio Class's own; a work viewer or printed cards. Free path: tea or coffee as a wash, pencil, scrap paper. Short path: 15 days | First paired unit because every later unit needs the safe-studio habits of this level (hazard cards before a material) and the looking routine applied to a set of works; unplanned making comes before the planned starts of unit 3 so students know what materials do | Candidate: an unplanned or process-led work by a living maker from Asia or the Americas | Hazards and controls: ink and watercolor on tables (covered tables, water cups not near hands); air-dry clay moist, not sanded; hands washed; ventilation on for ink. The Migration Series tells of segregation and violence in the American South: the sensitivity agent reads the Phillips Collection's and MoMA's pages and Lawrence's own captions first; panels showing violence are named and studied by choice. The everyday-images reflection uses images the student chooses, never a feed handed in, and no student's own photos of people. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | Level 1 | Starting points. *How do artists start, and whose standards should we use to judge unfinished work?* | 34 | va-g9-ideas; va-g9-revise | VA:Cr1.1.Ia; VA:Cr1.2.Ia; VA:Cr3.1.Ia | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review); connecting through the works' sources. Works: Wangechi Mutu, The NewOnes, will free Us (Kenya-born, works in the United States and Kenya; 2019, four bronze figures for the Metropolitan Museum of Art's facade; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; a caryatid from the Erechtheion, Athens (Greece, about 421 to 406 BCE, the British Museum), the old figure Mutu answers; Xie He's six principles of painting (China, about 500 CE), a past culture's named standards, given in our own summary | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the grade 8 ideas and revise rows (va-g8-ideas, va-g8-revise) and the bridge's critique routine. VA:Cr1.1.Ia: start one project three ways (a word web, a thumbnail page, a photo walk or viewfinder walk) and pick the strongest with a reason. VA:Cr1.2.Ia: frame a question about daily life today that a current artist or designer has also taken up, and shape the project around it. VA:Cr3.1.Ia: review and plan revisions of the unfinished work with standards from a past culture (Xie He's principles) and a present one (the class's own guide, or a living maker's stated aims), naming whose standards each is. Technique: thumbnails and a sustained observational study in pencil and ink. The first artist statement of the level, drafted, given written feedback and revised. Words: route, inquiry, contemporary, practice, standard, revise. Showing: the finished work beside its three starts and its revision plan, for another class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Cr1.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the planning portfolio entry: three start routes tried, the strongest chosen with a reason; VA:Cr1.2.Ia, a guide look-for on the same entry and the work: a question about daily life today, tied to a named current maker's practice; VA:Cr3.1.Ia, the critique record and the revised work, scored on the guide: two standards named as whose, a planned change, the change made, and a sitting item applying one named standard to an unseen unfinished work (a Curio Class original). Mid-unit assessment (day 17): the planning portfolio entry, with a respond day. Out: problems drawn from the student's own earlier work (Level 2). Tools: step cards, a summary card of Xie He's principles, work cards, the critique card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil, ink from a borrowed set, copy-paper sketchbook. Short path: 16 days | After unit 2 because a planned start needs what unplanned work showed about materials, and the revision uses the critique routine on a work in progress. Before unit 4 because the team proposal starts from this unit's start routes and idea pages | Candidate: a present-day standards set from outside Europe and North America, from its own published source | Mutu's figures answer caryatids, which hold up a building: the unit names the old use and her stated aims, from the Met's pages, read first by the sensitivity agent. Photo walk: no faces of people without their yes, school grounds only, the class together. Hazards and controls: ink on tables (covered), hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | Level 1 | Changing a place. *How can a design change the way people move and feel in one place, and what should a jury look for?* | 40 | va-g9-design; va-g9-judge | VA:Cr2.3.Ia; VA:Cn10.1.Ia; VA:Re8.1.Ia; VA:Re9.1.Ia | Creating (Cr2); connecting (Cn10); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Maya Lin, Vietnam Veterans Memorial (United States, 1982, Washington, DC, the National Park Service), the design a jury chose in a 1981 open competition from a student's entry, with the competition's published aims; Christo and Jeanne-Claude, The Gates (United States, 2005, Central Park, New York), a temporary work that changed a park, described and linked; Olowe of Ise, Veranda Post of Enthroned King and Senior Wife (Opo Ogoga) (Nigeria, Yoruba, 1910 to 1914, the Art Institute of Chicago, open access), carved posts that shaped a palace courtyard | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the grade 8 design and judge rows (va-g8-design, va-g8-judge) and unit 3's start routes. VA:Cr2.3.Ia: with a team of three or four, propose a change to one school space that would alter how people move or feel there, in plan drawings and a card scale model (scale drawing is grade 7 math, used here only as a given scale: 1 to 50). VA:Cn10.1.Ia: keep an idea log from first sketch to final proposal, each step dated. VA:Re8.1.Ia: write an interpretation of one of the three works, with enough evidence from the work and its setting. VA:Re9.1.Ia: draft the standards a jury would use to judge that work or a collection, beside the 1981 competition's aims. The written rationale for the proposal, drafted, given written feedback and revised. Words: propose, space, experience, evidence, context, standards. Showing: the proposals presented to a panel of two adults or a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Cr2.3.Ia, a guide look-for on the model and rationale: the place named, the change to movement or feeling shown, the student's part in the team named; VA:Cn10.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the idea log: at least four dated steps from first spark to the final proposal, with what changed at each; VA:Re8.1.Ia, a sitting item: an interpretation of an unseen open-licensed work with three pieces of evidence from the work and one from its setting; VA:Re9.1.Ia, a sitting item: three standards set for judging that work, each with a reason it fits. Mid-unit assessment (day 20): the idea log to date and the first plan drawing, with a respond day. Out: redesign in answer to an issue (Level 2). Tools: a design-brief card, a scale ruler card, work cards, a reading on the memorial competition, Curio Class's own; an exhibit floor-plan tool or its printed grid. Free path: cereal-box card, tape, pencil. Short path: 20 days | After unit 3 because the proposal starts from the start routes and the idea log grows from the planning pages; judging needs unit 2's looking routine and adds evidence and standards. Before unit 5 because curating a show uses this unit's standards for a collection. Longest Level 1 unit: a team build, a log, a rationale and two written items | Candidate: a team-design case from Asia or Latin America (a public space changed by design), from an open source | Proposals are drawings and models; nothing is built or changed on school grounds without permission (va-g9-design note). The memorial lists the names of the dead of the Vietnam War: the unit tells the design and its jury, the sensitivity agent reads the National Park Service's pages first, and a student with a family tie may choose another work. Team work: each student's part is named on the log, and a solo version exists. Hazards and controls: card and tape only, no blades (an adult cuts any thick card), glue sticks. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | Level 1 | The curator's eye. *How does the way a show is laid out shape what visitors believe, and how does knowing a culture change what we see?* | 38 | va-g9-show; va-g9-museum | VA:Pr4.1.Ia; VA:Pr5.1.Ia; VA:Pr6.1.Ia; VA:Cn11.1.Ia | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn11); responding practiced on the exhibitions (review). Works and shows: Fred Wilson, Mining the Museum (United States, 1992 to 1993, the Maryland Historical Society, Baltimore, now the Maryland Center for History and Culture), a show that set the museum's own objects side by side to tell what its displays had left out; the National Museum of the American Indian's exhibition Americans (Washington, DC, opened 2018), on images of Native people in American life; Ai Weiwei, Sunflower Seeds (China, 2010, Tate; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), porcelain seeds made by hand in Jingdezhen, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the grade 8 show and museum rows (va-g8-show, va-g8-museum) and unit 4's standards. VA:Pr4.1.Ia: analyze, choose and curate a small show of the class's Level 1 work, and choose what to keep and how (a kept collection with its care notes). VA:Pr5.1.Ia: analyze and judge, in a short written note, why and how a real exhibition is laid out (Mining the Museum or Americans, from the museum's own pages and our reading). VA:Pr6.1.Ia: analyze how that exhibit or collection shapes the student's awareness of society, culture and politics. VA:Cn11.1.Ia: describe how knowing a culture, its customs and its history can change a viewer's reaction to a work (Sunflower Seeds before and after the reading on Jingdezhen and the seeds' history). Technique: mounting, label writing from the artist statement, a floor plan. Words: curate, exhibition, analyze, society, custom, reaction. Showing: the class show for families or the school, with each family's yes, a student of 18 giving their own. End-of-unit evidence (two check days; the show is the unit's own end-of-unit assessment), id by id: VA:Pr4.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the exhibition plan and the show: works chosen and ordered with written reasons, and the kept collection's care notes; VA:Pr5.1.Ia, the kept note on a real exhibition's layout, scored on its guide (two reasons and two ways named, a judgment of whether they work); VA:Pr6.1.Ia, a sitting item: how an unseen exhibit's room plan and labels (a Curio Class original) shape a visitor's awareness of a social or political question; VA:Cn11.1.Ia, a sitting item: an unseen work with a short reading on its culture, the first reaction and how the reading changed it. Mid-unit assessment (day 19): the exhibition plan draft, with a respond day. Out: a portfolio show sited in one place (Level 2). Tools: the exhibit-plan card, label cards, work cards, readings on the two exhibitions and on Jingdezhen porcelain, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box mounts, tape, a hallway wall. Short path: 19 days | Last in Level 1 because the show is curated from the level's kept work (units 1 to 4) and the analysis of real exhibitions uses unit 4's standards for a collection. The year-end check (week 32) follows it and samples units 2 to 4 | Candidate: an exhibition from Africa or Asia whose own pages explain its layout | Mining the Museum showed slave shackles beside fine silver: the unit names it as a show about slavery and its record, the sensitivity agent reads the Maryland Center for History and Culture's and the artist's own published words first, and a student may write on Americans instead. Americans: the sensitivity agent reads the museum's own pages first; the unit studies how images of Native people are used, never imitates them. Ai Weiwei's work touches Chinese politics: facts from the holding museum, no claim beyond the sources. Family yes, or the student's own at 18, for any showing outside class. Hazards and controls: an adult hangs work above head height; no ladders for students; glue sticks and tape. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
