| 7 | Level 2 | Images that move people. *How well do images sway what an audience thinks, feels and does, and what do we owe the people and makers in our images?* | 34 | va-g10-look; va-g10-craft | VA:Re7.1.IIa; VA:Re7.2.IIa; VA:Cr2.1.IIa; VA:Cr2.2.IIa | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2). Works: Dorothea Lange, Migrant Mother, Nipomo, California (United States, 1936, the Library of Congress, public domain), a photograph made for a government agency; Shepard Fairey, Barack Obama "Hope" poster (United States, 2008, the National Portrait Gallery), made from a news photograph, and the court case over its use (settled 2011), described and linked; Thomas Cole, View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm (The Oxbow) (United States, 1836, the Metropolitan Museum of Art, open access), a painted reaction to land and its clearing | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's look and craft rows (va-g9-look, va-g9-craft). VA:Re7.1.IIa: keep a page of the student's own aesthetic and empathetic reactions to natural and built places (a walk on school grounds, The Oxbow). VA:Re7.2.IIa: judge how well a set of images (Lange's photograph and the agency's other 1930s photographs, the poster) sways a named audience's ideas, feelings and actions. VA:Cr2.1.IIa: show skill and knowledge gained in one chosen medium (drawing, ink wash or foam-plate print) through experiments and persistence, documented in the practice log over the unit. VA:Cr2.2.IIa: show understanding of the ethics of making and sharing creative work (credit, consent, cultural care), reasoned on the two cases: the sitter's consent in 1936, the news photograph's credit in 2008. Words: aesthetic, empathy, audience, persistence, ethics, consent. Showing: the medium studies and the reaction page, with credit lines, for the class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Re7.1.IIa, a guide look-for on the reaction page: four places, each with an aesthetic and an empathetic reaction described; VA:Re7.2.IIa, a sitting item: an unseen public domain poster, its audience named, and a judgment of how well it sways their ideas, feelings and actions, with evidence; VA:Cr2.1.IIa, a guide look-for on the practice log and the last study: experiments dated, a skill named and shown grown from the first study; VA:Cr2.2.IIa, a sitting item: a short unseen case (a Curio Class original) on making and sharing an image, the ethical stakes named and a reasoned choice. Mid-unit assessment (day 17): the practice log and the first two studies, with a respond day. Out: liberty weighed against duty (Level 3). Tools: work cards, case cards on the two images, a reading on the 1930s agency photographs, step cards for foam-plate printing, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil and a ballpoint for the foam-plate, plates from clean food trays. Short path: 16 days | First paired unit of Level 2 because its practice log documents skill growth through the rest of the level and the ethics of sharing come before the student's own work is shown in units 9 and 10 | Candidate: an image from Asia or Africa that swayed an audience, public domain, with its setting | Migrant Mother: Florence Owens Thompson and her family later spoke of how the photograph was made and used; the sensitivity agent reads the Library of Congress's pages and the family's published words first, and the unit teaches her with dignity, by name. The poster is a political portrait: the unit studies its making and the credit question, never a side; values rule. Hazards and controls: water-based printing ink (non-toxic label), brayers, covered tables, hands washed; foam plates drawn with a ballpoint, no blades. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 8 | Level 2 | Problems from my own work. *What problem did my last work leave open, and which materials and methods, old and new, will solve it?* | 36 | va-g10-ideas; va-g10-revise | VA:Cr1.1.IIa; VA:Cr1.2.IIa; VA:Cr3.1.IIa | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Bisa Butler, The Safety Patrol (United States, 2018, the Art Institute of Chicago), a quilted portrait made from an old photograph, described and linked; Hung Liu, Resident Alien (China-born, worked in the United States, 1988, the San Jose Museum of Art; 1948 to 2021), a painting of her own immigration card, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's ideas and revise rows (va-g9-ideas, va-g9-revise). VA:Cr1.1.IIa: pose a fresh problem drawn from a piece of the student's own earlier work, alone or with a partner. VA:Cr1.2.IIa: fill a planning page that weighs two or three materials and methods, old and new (quilting or collage, ink, mixed media), before choosing. VA:Cr3.1.IIa: after a classmate critique, reflect, rework and polish the work toward the student's own vision, keeping the draft and the final side by side. The artist statement, drafted, given written feedback and revised. Words: pose, problem, method, vision, polish, draft. Showing: draft and final side by side with the statement, for another class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Cr1.1.IIa, a guide look-for on the planning portfolio entry: the earlier work named and the open problem stated; VA:Cr1.2.IIa, a guide look-for on the planning page: two or three materials and methods weighed, one old and one new, the choice reasoned; VA:Cr3.1.IIa, the critique record, the draft and the final, scored on the guide: the peer's point, the reflection, the rework and what the vision asked for, and a sitting item: an unseen draft and final pair (a Curio Class original), what changed and whether it serves a stated vision. Mid-unit assessment (day 18): the planning portfolio entry, with a respond day. Out: a themed series and a plan for social change (Level 3). Tools: the planning page card, step cards for fabric collage, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: fabric scraps from home or a thrift bin, glue, paper. Short path: 17 days | After unit 7 because the problem comes from earlier work, and the practice log shows what each medium can do. Before unit 9 because the redesign uses the planning page and the polished revision habit | Candidate: an old method made new by a living maker from Africa or the Pacific | Resident Alien shows an immigration card: the unit tells it from the museum's and the artist's estate's pages, read first by the sensitivity agent; no student is asked about their own or a family member's immigration status. Hazards and controls: blunt fabric scissors, pins counted out and back, glue sticks or white glue; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 9 | Level 2 | Redesign for now. *How can a redesign answer an issue of today, and what do we need to know before we judge a work?* | 40 | va-g10-design; va-g10-judge | VA:Cr2.3.IIa; VA:Cn10.1.IIa; VA:Re8.1.IIa; VA:Re9.1.IIa | Creating (Cr2); connecting (Cn10); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Theaster Gates, Stony Island Arts Bank (United States, opened 2015, Chicago), a closed bank redesigned as a library and arts space, described and linked; Cannupa Hanska Luger (Mandan, Hidatsa, Arikara and Lakota), Mirror Shield Project (United States, 2016, Standing Rock, North Dakota; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), shields redesigned from mirrored board, described and linked from the artist's own pages; Wendy Red Star (ApsA�alooke, Crow), 1880 Crow Peace Delegation (United States, 2014; the unit plan records whether living and the holding museum), portraits annotated with the facts their first viewers lacked, described and linked; revisit of Lin's memorial (unit 4) and its critics in 1982 | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's design and judge rows (va-g9-design, va-g9-judge). VA:Cr2.3.IIa: redesign an object, a routine or a place in answer to a current issue the student chooses from the teacher's checked list (or an invented one), in drawings and a model. VA:Cn10.1.IIa: explore a subject new to the student through observation, research and experiment, kept on a research page of what was observed, read and tested while making it. VA:Re8.1.IIa: list the kinds of background facts that change how a work is read (the maker, the time, the first audience, the place), using Red Star's annotations. VA:Re9.1.IIa: test how well two other people's judging standards fit one work: the 1981 memorial competition's aims and a 1982 critic's objections, tried against Lin's memorial. The written rationale, drafted, given written feedback and revised. Words: rework, issue, investigate, background, interpretation, fit. Showing: the redesigns presented with their research pages to a panel or a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Cr2.3.IIa, a guide look-for on the model and rationale: the issue named, the redesign answering it, the audience; VA:Cn10.1.IIa, a guide look-for on the research page: what was observed, read and tested, each dated; VA:Re8.1.IIa, a sitting item: an unseen work with its label, the kinds of background facts that would change its reading, named and sorted; VA:Re9.1.IIa, a sitting item: two given sets of standards (Curio Class originals) tested against an unseen work, which fits better and why. Mid-unit assessment (day 20): the research page and first drawings, with a respond day. Out: a work arguing how a culture's images help or hinder lives (Level 3). Tools: the design-brief card, the research page, case cards, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box card, tape, found objects. Short path: 20 days | After unit 8 because the redesign uses the planning page and polished revision; judging others' standards builds on Level 1's standards set (unit 4), revisited here. Before unit 10 because the arguments about art and history there need the background-facts list | Candidate: a redesign case from Asia or Latin America, from its own published source | Mirror Shield: made for the 2016 water protectors at Standing Rock; the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own words and a Standing Rock Sioux Tribe or Native-run source first; the unit studies the design, never takes a side on the pipeline (values rule), and no student copies Native designs. Red Star's work is about her own nation's delegation: her own published words first. Issues: the student picks from a checked list or an invented case, marked the same; the side is never scored. Hazards and controls: card and tape, an adult cuts any thick card or board, no mirrors or glass (foil card instead). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 10 | Level 2 | Art, power and place. *How has art served power in past societies and here today, and how do we show our own work in one real place?* | 40 | va-g10-show; va-g10-museum | VA:Pr4.1.IIa; VA:Pr5.1.IIa; VA:Pr6.1.IIa; VA:Cn11.1.IIa | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn11); responding practiced (review). Works: a marble portrait of the emperor Augustus (Roman, about 1st century, the Metropolitan Museum of Art, open access; the unit plan picks the object); a portrait of the emperor Shah Jahan from the Shah Jahan Album (Mughal India, about 1620s to 1630s, the Metropolitan Museum of Art, open access; the unit plan picks the leaf); Kehinde Wiley, Barack Obama (United States, 2018, the National Portrait Gallery), described and linked; Yinka Shonibare, Nelson's Ship in a Bottle (British-Nigerian, 2010, Trafalgar Square's Fourth Plinth, now the National Maritime Museum, Greenwich; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's show and museum rows (va-g9-show, va-g9-museum). VA:Pr4.1.IIa: analyze, choose and critique the student's own Level 2 works for a portfolio show. VA:Pr5.1.IIa: judge, choose and use hanging and lighting methods that suit one named place in the school (a hallway, the library, a stairwell), using the light the place already has. VA:Pr6.1.IIa: write an argument that ties a set of works (Shonibare's ship and its wax-print sails) to the history, culture and politics around them, and defend the links. VA:Cn11.1.IIa: compare how art was used in two past societies (Rome, Mughal India: rulers' portraits) with how it is used locally now (an official portrait, a local mural or monument). Words: portfolio, site, method, link, defend, local. Showing: the portfolio show in the named place, for families and the school, each family's yes or the student's own at 18. End-of-unit evidence (two check days; the show is the unit's own end-of-unit assessment), id by id: VA:Pr4.1.IIa, a guide look-for on the portfolio selection: works chosen and critiqued in writing, one left out with a reason; VA:Pr5.1.IIa, the exhibition plan for the named place, scored on its guide (methods weighed, chosen and used, the place's light and traffic named); VA:Pr6.1.IIa, the kept argument, scored on its guide: links argued and defended with sources; VA:Cn11.1.IIa, a sitting item: two unseen images from two past societies (open access) compared for their use, linked to one local use today. Mid-unit assessment (day 20): the argument's draft with written feedback, with a respond day. Out: an event show with conservation, and a judged influence (Level 3). Tools: the exhibit-plan card, a hanging-methods card, work cards, readings on the Roman and Mughal portraits and on wax print's trade history, Curio Class's own. Free path: tape, string and clips, a hallway wall. Short path: 20 days | Last in Level 2 because the portfolio show is chosen from the level's kept work and the arguments use unit 9's background facts. The year-end check (week 32) follows it and samples units 7 to 9 | Candidate: a local mural or monument named by the school, with its own maker's or city's page | Augustus, Shah Jahan and the official portrait are rulers' images: the unit studies how art served power, with facts from the holding museums. Shonibare's work ties to British naval history and colonial trade: the sensitivity agent reads the Royal Museums Greenwich page and the artist's own words first. Local monuments may be disputed: facts and sources decide claims (values rule). Hazards and controls: an adult hangs work above head height and handles any light; no ladders for students. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
