Write an interpretation of an artwork backed with evidence from the piece and its setting, and draft the standards a jury would use to judge that work or a collection. What changes: evidence is counted for enough, and the student sets standards for a work or collection. Assessed by a written judgment that sets standards, applies them and gives evidence, scored on a rubric.

va-g10-judge | VA:Re8.1.IIa; VA:Re9.1.IIa
List the background facts that change how an artwork is read, then test how well two other people's judging standards fit one work or collection. What changes: other people's standards are tested against a work, and background that helps interpretation is named. Assessed by a written judgment that sets standards, applies them and gives evidence, scored on a rubric.

va-g11-judge | VA:Re8.1.IIIa; VA:Re9.1.IIIa
Lay out competing readings of an artwork and defend the most believable, then judge one work or collection by several different sets of standards. What changes: competing interpretations are weighed against each other and several sets of standards are used in one judgment. Assessed by a written judgment that sets standards, applies them and gives evidence, scored on a rubric.

va-g12-studio | ~VA:Cr1.1.IIIa; ~VA:Cr1.2.IIIa; ~VA:Cr2.1.IIIa; ~VA:Cr3.1.IIIa
Level 4 is an optional year the student leads: a self-directed series built from a written proposal, with planning, making and revision repeated on new work. Assessed by a portfolio of the series and a defended artist statement, scored on the same rubric as Level 3 with a higher bar for independence. The Level 3 ids are owned in grade 11; this year applies them to new, self-chosen work.

va-g12-purpose | ~VA:Cr2.2.IIIa; ~VA:Cr2.3.IIIa; ~VA:Cn10.1.IIIa
The student takes on one project that uses art or design to speak to a real audience, and keeps a record of choices about materials, sources, consent and sharing. Assessed by the finished project and a written account of its ethical choices. The Level 3 ids are owned in grade 11; this year applies them to a new project.

va-g12-exhibit | ~VA:Pr4.1.IIIa; ~VA:Pr5.1.IIIa; ~VA:Pr6.1.IIIa; ~VA:Cn11.1.IIIa
The student curates and presents a small public exhibit from the Level 4 work, writing a justified label for each piece and a plan for protecting the work. Assessed by the exhibition, the labels and a short talk to visitors, scored on a rubric. Teacher note: a showing outside class needs each family's yes. A student of 18 or over gives their own yes to be shown; otherwise the family does. The Level 3 ids are owned in grade 11.

va-g12-criticism | ~VA:Re7.1.IIIa; ~VA:Re7.2.IIIa; ~VA:Re8.1.IIIa; ~VA:Re9.1.IIIa
The student writes an extended critical essay comparing the interpretations of a work or group of works, defending the most believable one with evidence and more than one set of standards. Assessed by the essay and a defended talk, scored on a rubric. The Level 3 ids are owned in grade 11; this year applies them to new works.

