# The Curio Class approach to teaching

This page says how Curio Class teaches, in every subject, and why. It is layer 3 of a course: it is built on
[FOUNDATION.md](FOUNDATION.md) and on the standards, and every scope and sequence and section plan is built on it. Each subject
adds its own page, which says how the subject carries out each principle. Mathematics and English language arts have pages,
[APPROACH-MATH.md](APPROACH-MATH.md) and [APPROACH-ELA.md](APPROACH-ELA.md); for science and social studies,
the subject lens (docs/agents/lenses/) plus its rubric stands in through the scopes and sequences and unit plans, and
a short approach page per subject is written before days (Cory, 2026-11-14). The subjects after the core have pages
in their course folders: [health](courses/health/APPROACH.md),
[physical education](courses/physical-education/APPROACH.md), [arts](courses/arts/APPROACH.md),
[computer science](courses/computer-science/APPROACH.md), [social and emotional learning](courses/sel/APPROACH.md),
[library and research skills](courses/library/APPROACH.md), [English language development](courses/eld/APPROACH.md),
[career education](courses/career-education/APPROACH.md) and
[world languages](courses/world-languages/APPROACH.md) (shelved).

Two honest notes. Nothing in Curio Class has been tried with children yet, so this page makes no claim about
results; it explains choices, and its rules are rules for section plans, not descriptions of lessons that exist.
And every claim about how students learn rests on a source opened and read for this page. The list at the end
says how much of each was read. Where a federal practice guide rates its own evidence (strong, moderate, or
minimal; the oldest guide says low), that rating is given. The detail behind each claim (who was studied, how
many studies, how big the effect) is in [APPROACH-SOURCES.md](APPROACH-SOURCES.md), "Evidence notes".

Second draft, 2026-11-11, revised after a cold read by the curriculum expert
([reviews/approach-2026-11-11.md](reviews/approach-2026-11-11.md)). Cory settled three points the same day
(how a lesson opens, no timers in tools, narrowed on 2026-11-14 to timers with a grounded purpose, BASELINE rule 30; a wording change in SPEC.md). The page as a whole still waits for his
read.

Changed 2026-11-12, Cory's yes on card q-ela-piece-2: principle 3 and the modelling line under "Where the
evidence is thin or contested" now give the federal ratings for reading and writing
([reviews/approach-ela-2026-11-11.md](reviews/approach-ela-2026-11-11.md), finding F4).

> **What this means in your room, or at your table**
>
> - We find out what your student already knows before teaching something new.
> - New things are shown clearly, step by step, with examples. Then the help is taken away, on purpose.
> - A lesson may open with a short question or task. The teaching always follows the same day.
> - Old work keeps coming back, from memory, so it is not forgotten.
> - Every day leaves you something you can act on: what was understood, and what to do if it was not.
> - Students talk and write about what they learn. You judge that work; we give you what you need to judge it.
> - A student who is behind works on the grade's content with extra support, not on easier content.
> - A screen is used where it helps, and real things are used where the idea lives in real things.
> - If you are teaching one child, every unit has a note for you.

## The role of the standards

The standards decide **what** a student should know and be able to do by the end of a grade, and in what words.
A Curio Class check uses the standard's own verb, so "explain" is not quietly swapped for "pick".

The standards leave the teaching to us. The mathematics standards say so directly: they "do not dictate
curriculum or teaching methods" and do not define the supports for students who are behind, ahead, or learning
English (Common Core State Standards for Mathematics, introduction). Each subject page says the same for its
own standards from its own standards document. So the order of the year, how each idea is taught, the practice,
the checks and the supports are our choices, and this page and the subject pages are where they are explained.

### Which standards: national, not federal (from the Phase 1 scope, 2026-10-22)

There are no federal K-12 content standards. Federal law (ESSA, and earlier the Department of Education Organization Act) bars the federal government from setting curriculum, and each state adopts its own standards. The highest level that exists is the **national consensus framework** for each subject, written by states, teachers or professional associations. Most state standards are adopted from or modeled on these. That is our alignment target everywhere, which keeps us from tying the site to any one state.

Where a subject has no national framework (Precalculus, Statistics, Calculus), the top level is the College Board AP course framework, which every state recognizes.

| Subject | Framework (version) | Code level in our data | Example | Verified against |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Math | Common Core State Standards for Mathematics (2010) | cluster | `3.NF.A`, `A-REI.B.4` | Cluster list per grade and domain from corestandards.org (from memory, spot-checked) |
| ELA | Common Core ELA (2010) | standard | `RL.4.2`, `RF.K.1` | Strand rules: RL/RI/W 1-10, SL/L 1-6, RF only K-5 (RF.1-2 only K-1), no RL.8 |
| Science | NGSS (2013) | performance expectation | `MS-PS1-4` | Every PE code in the official NGSS All-DCI PDF (exact match) |
| Social Studies | C3 Framework (2013, rev. 2017) | Dimension 2 indicator | `D2.His.14.6-8` | Indicator list from the official C3 PDF |
| Arts | National Core Arts Standards (2014) | discipline + anchor | `VA:Cr2`, `MU:Re7` | 11 anchors x 5 disciplines |
| Health | National Health Education Standards (3rd ed., 2024) | standard 1-8 | `NHES.S3` | SHAPE America 2024 release |
| PE | SHAPE America National PE Standards (2024) | standard 1-4 | `SHAPE.S2` | 2024 release (cut from 5 to 4 standards) |
| Computer Science | **2026 CSTA PK-12 CS Standards** (released July 2026) | standard | `E3-PRO-VD-06`, `MS-SYS-NT-34` | All 196 identifiers extracted from the official 2026 PDF |
| World Languages | ACTFL Proficiency Guidelines; World-Readiness Standards | level / goal area | `ACTFL.Novice Mid`, `WRS.Cultures` | Closed list |
| CTE | Advance CTE Modernized Career Clusters (2024, 14 clusters) | framework | `CTE.Career Clusters` | 2024 framework (replaced 16 clusters) |
| Personal Finance | National Standards for Personal Financial Education (2021) | standard area | `NSPFE.Managing Credit` | 6 areas |
| Psychology | APA National Standards for HS Psychology | pillar | `APA.Cognition` | Pillar names |
| SEL | CASEL framework | competency | `CASEL.Self-Management` | 5 competencies |
| English learners | WIDA ELD Standards Framework (2020) | standard + key language use | `WIDA.ELD-4`, `WIDA.Argue` | 5 standards, 4 key language uses |
